1
00:00:01,240 --> 00:00:03,910
On November eighteenth, twenty twenty-six,

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,729
a machine about the size of a small car crosses

3
00:00:06,749 --> 00:00:10,069
a line that nothing humans have built has ever crossed.

4
00:00:11,149 --> 00:00:13,419
It becomes one light-day away from Earth.

5
00:00:13,789 --> 00:00:15,609
Say hello to it at that moment,

6
00:00:15,669 --> 00:00:16,519
and your words,

7
00:00:16,539 --> 00:00:18,049
flying at the speed of light,

8
00:00:18,069 --> 00:00:19,659
need a whole day to get there.

9
00:00:19,949 --> 00:00:22,419
Its answer needs another day to come back.

10
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:25,719
That machine is Voyager one.

11
00:00:26,019 --> 00:00:28,429
It was launched in nineteen seventy-seven.

12
00:00:28,749 --> 00:00:30,336
Its radio is about as strong as

13
00:00:30,356 --> 00:00:32,089
the light bulb in your fridge,

14
00:00:32,219 --> 00:00:34,447
and each of its computers has less memory

15
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:36,655
than a single photo on your phone.

16
00:00:37,845 --> 00:00:38,951
And yet, right now,

17
00:00:38,971 --> 00:00:40,587
it is still talking to us.

18
00:00:40,607 --> 00:00:43,986
Every day, giant dishes on Earth catch its whisper.

19
00:00:44,876 --> 00:00:46,186
By the end of this video,

20
00:00:46,226 --> 00:00:49,136
you'll know how a whisper that faint crosses a light-day,

21
00:00:49,386 --> 00:00:52,096
what keeps a forty-nine-year-old machine alive,

22
00:00:52,276 --> 00:00:54,878
and what it carries that may outlast all of us.

23
00:00:55,468 --> 00:00:57,897
So, how do we still hear Voyager one?

24
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:05,707
Chapter one.

25
00:01:05,727 --> 00:01:06,910
What is a light-day?

26
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:09,760
A light-day sounds like a length of time.

27
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:11,420
It's actually a distance.

28
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:12,845
And a ridiculous one.

29
00:01:13,445 --> 00:01:15,675
Light is the fastest thing there is.

30
00:01:15,915 --> 00:01:19,618
It covers almost three hundred thousand kilometres every second.

31
00:01:20,218 --> 00:01:22,004
Light from the Moon reaches you in

32
00:01:22,024 --> 00:01:23,908
about one and a quarter seconds.

33
00:01:24,238 --> 00:01:25,782
Light from the Sun takes about

34
00:01:25,802 --> 00:01:27,735
eight minutes and twenty seconds.

35
00:01:28,825 --> 00:01:31,895
Now let light run for a full day, without stopping.

36
00:01:32,105 --> 00:01:33,475
Twenty-four hours.

37
00:01:33,615 --> 00:01:37,057
It covers about twenty-five point nine billion kilometres.

38
00:01:38,237 --> 00:01:40,698
That's about a hundred and seventy-three times the

39
00:01:40,718 --> 00:01:42,894
distance from the Earth to the Sun.

40
00:01:43,984 --> 00:01:47,412
Nothing humans have ever made has been that far away.

41
00:01:47,432 --> 00:01:48,397
Not one rocket,

42
00:01:48,417 --> 00:01:49,327
not one probe.

43
00:01:49,347 --> 00:01:50,359
Until now.

44
00:01:51,339 --> 00:01:53,708
Voyager one needed forty-nine years of

45
00:01:53,728 --> 00:01:55,769
non-stop flight to get there.

46
00:01:56,019 --> 00:01:57,549
Which raises a question.

47
00:01:57,869 --> 00:01:59,198
How did a machine from the

48
00:01:59,218 --> 00:02:00,989
nineteen seventies get so far?

49
00:02:02,169 --> 00:02:03,059
Chapter two.

50
00:02:03,079 --> 00:02:04,565
The cosmic slingshot.

51
00:02:05,265 --> 00:02:07,095
Here's a myth you've probably heard.

52
00:02:07,245 --> 00:02:10,355
Voyager one is the fastest spacecraft ever built.

53
00:02:10,535 --> 00:02:11,331
It isn't.

54
00:02:11,921 --> 00:02:13,671
In the late nineteen seventies,

55
00:02:13,831 --> 00:02:16,652
the giant outer planets lined up in a way that

56
00:02:16,672 --> 00:02:20,430
happens only about once every hundred and seventy-five years.

57
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:23,623
Voyager two launched first, in August.

58
00:02:23,643 --> 00:02:26,367
Voyager one followed on September the fifth,

59
00:02:26,387 --> 00:02:27,497
on a faster path,

60
00:02:27,517 --> 00:02:29,272
so it reached Jupiter first.

61
00:02:29,292 --> 00:02:31,727
That's how it got the number one.

62
00:02:32,817 --> 00:02:33,927
Then came the trick.

63
00:02:34,297 --> 00:02:37,267
Swinging past Jupiter in nineteen seventy-nine,

64
00:02:37,307 --> 00:02:38,957
and Saturn in nineteen eighty,

65
00:02:39,057 --> 00:02:41,977
it borrowed a tiny bit of each planet's motion,

66
00:02:42,057 --> 00:02:43,337
and was flung outward,

67
00:02:43,427 --> 00:02:44,782
faster than before.

68
00:02:45,962 --> 00:02:49,582
Today it moves at about sixty-one thousand kilometres an hour.

69
00:02:49,962 --> 00:02:51,652
Fast, but not a record.

70
00:02:52,002 --> 00:02:53,763
The Parker Solar Probe has gone

71
00:02:53,783 --> 00:02:55,532
more than ten times faster,

72
00:02:55,622 --> 00:02:57,105
diving toward the Sun.

73
00:02:58,295 --> 00:03:00,695
Voyager's real secret isn't speed.

74
00:03:00,955 --> 00:03:01,785
It's time.

75
00:03:01,955 --> 00:03:03,015
It has never stopped, not once,

76
00:03:03,035 --> 00:03:05,054
in forty-nine years.

77
00:03:06,034 --> 00:03:07,398
Its twin, Voyager two,

78
00:03:07,418 --> 00:03:09,502
is billions of kilometres behind.

79
00:03:09,522 --> 00:03:11,550
So Voyager one is the farthest

80
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:13,211
thing we have ever built.

81
00:03:13,231 --> 00:03:14,761
But from that far away,

82
00:03:14,781 --> 00:03:17,838
how does its tiny voice reach us at all?

83
00:03:19,018 --> 00:03:19,938
Chapter three.

84
00:03:19,958 --> 00:03:21,670
A whisper across a light-day.

85
00:03:22,390 --> 00:03:24,710
Here's the part that sounds impossible.

86
00:03:25,050 --> 00:03:27,377
Voyager's radio is roughly as powerful

87
00:03:27,397 --> 00:03:29,695
as the bulb inside your fridge.

88
00:03:30,285 --> 00:03:31,800
It aims that power with a

89
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:34,035
dish three point seven metres wide,

90
00:03:34,285 --> 00:03:36,160
pointed at a planet far too small

91
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:38,047
and far too distant to see.

92
00:03:38,627 --> 00:03:40,837
But the beam spreads out as it travels.

93
00:03:41,087 --> 00:03:43,621
NASA once put the signal that reaches Earth at a

94
00:03:43,641 --> 00:03:46,357
tenth of a billionth of a trillionth of a watt.

95
00:03:46,647 --> 00:03:48,597
Today, it's even weaker.

96
00:03:49,807 --> 00:03:51,788
To catch something that faint,

97
00:03:51,808 --> 00:03:53,161
we built giant ears.

98
00:03:53,181 --> 00:03:56,879
NASA's Deep Space Network has dishes seventy metres wide,

99
00:03:56,899 --> 00:03:58,423
in California, in Spain,

100
00:03:58,443 --> 00:03:59,938
and in Australia.

101
00:04:00,928 --> 00:04:02,091
They sit about a third of the

102
00:04:02,111 --> 00:04:03,768
way around the world from each other,

103
00:04:03,948 --> 00:04:05,338
so as Earth turns,

104
00:04:05,438 --> 00:04:07,751
one of them can always face deep space.

105
00:04:08,341 --> 00:04:10,141
And the data arrives slowly.

106
00:04:10,351 --> 00:04:12,441
A hundred and sixty bits per second.

107
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:14,392
One photo from your phone would take

108
00:04:14,412 --> 00:04:16,274
almost two days to come through.

109
00:04:17,354 --> 00:04:19,178
Talking back is even harder.

110
00:04:19,198 --> 00:04:20,407
To send a command,

111
00:04:20,427 --> 00:04:24,721
engineers need their most powerful dish, in Canberra, Australia.

112
00:04:25,301 --> 00:04:26,621
So we can still hear it.

113
00:04:26,831 --> 00:04:28,491
But a radio needs power,

114
00:04:28,571 --> 00:04:30,303
and Voyager has been running on

115
00:04:30,323 --> 00:04:32,511
the same supply for forty-nine years.

116
00:04:32,771 --> 00:04:34,097
What keeps it alive?

117
00:04:35,277 --> 00:04:36,207
Chapter four.

118
00:04:36,227 --> 00:04:37,801
A slowly fading heart.

119
00:04:38,491 --> 00:04:40,551
Voyager has no solar panels.

120
00:04:40,751 --> 00:04:43,650
Out there, the Sun is just a very bright star.

121
00:04:44,230 --> 00:04:49,100
Instead, it runs on heat.

122
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:50,671
Three generators filled with plutonium turn the

123
00:04:50,691 --> 00:04:52,642
warmth of slowly decaying atoms into electricity.

124
00:04:53,232 --> 00:04:56,152
At launch, they made about four hundred and seventy watts.

125
00:04:56,472 --> 00:04:57,532
Every year since,

126
00:04:57,552 --> 00:04:59,799
Voyager has lost about four more.

127
00:05:00,779 --> 00:05:02,050
Today it lives on less than

128
00:05:02,070 --> 00:05:03,649
half of what it started with.

129
00:05:04,069 --> 00:05:05,783
Picture running your whole house on

130
00:05:05,803 --> 00:05:07,249
a couple of light bulbs,

131
00:05:07,329 --> 00:05:09,426
and losing a little more every year.

132
00:05:10,616 --> 00:05:12,656
So the team rations every watt.

133
00:05:12,896 --> 00:05:14,306
Heaters have been switched off,

134
00:05:14,326 --> 00:05:16,586
and the spacecraft keeps getting colder.

135
00:05:17,166 --> 00:05:19,436
It launched with ten science instruments.

136
00:05:19,626 --> 00:05:21,716
One by one,

137
00:05:21,986 --> 00:05:23,626
they have been turned off.

138
00:05:23,646 --> 00:05:24,796
In April twenty twenty-six,

139
00:05:24,816 --> 00:05:25,194
another one went dark.

140
00:05:26,174 --> 00:05:29,784
Today, Voyager one has just two instruments still working.

141
00:05:30,064 --> 00:05:32,084
One measures magnetic fields.

142
00:05:32,294 --> 00:05:33,748
The other listens to waves in

143
00:05:33,768 --> 00:05:35,311
the thin gas around it.

144
00:05:36,401 --> 00:05:37,916
NASA hopes at least one can

145
00:05:37,936 --> 00:05:39,991
keep going into the twenty-thirties.

146
00:05:40,301 --> 00:05:42,291
But what happens when something breaks,

147
00:05:42,371 --> 00:05:44,569
a light-day from the nearest mechanic?

148
00:05:45,749 --> 00:05:46,749
Chapter five.

149
00:05:46,769 --> 00:05:48,529
Repairs from a light-day away.

150
00:05:49,209 --> 00:05:51,619
Imagine fixing a computer you can't see,

151
00:05:51,769 --> 00:05:54,319
built before many of its engineers were born,

152
00:05:54,429 --> 00:05:56,490
where every test takes two days.

153
00:05:57,070 --> 00:05:58,790
In November twenty twenty-three,

154
00:05:58,850 --> 00:06:01,600
Voyager one suddenly started sending nonsense.

155
00:06:01,850 --> 00:06:03,413
Gibberish, for months.

156
00:06:03,983 --> 00:06:06,543
The team traced it to a single failed chip,

157
00:06:06,723 --> 00:06:09,845
holding about three percent of one computer's memory.

158
00:06:10,855 --> 00:06:11,965
There was no spare.

159
00:06:12,085 --> 00:06:13,905
So in April twenty twenty-four,

160
00:06:13,925 --> 00:06:15,888
they split the code into pieces and moved

161
00:06:15,908 --> 00:06:17,785
it into other parts of the memory.

162
00:06:18,135 --> 00:06:19,015
Two days later,

163
00:06:19,035 --> 00:06:20,345
the answer came back.

164
00:06:20,535 --> 00:06:21,413
It worked.

165
00:06:22,693 --> 00:06:28,513
Months later, a thruster fuel tube started clogging.

166
00:06:28,533 --> 00:06:29,541
It had narrowed from a quarter of a

167
00:06:29,561 --> 00:06:31,353
millimetre to just thirty-five thousandths of a millimetre.

168
00:06:31,673 --> 00:06:33,814
So the team switched to another set.

169
00:06:34,884 --> 00:06:36,274
In twenty twenty-five,

170
00:06:36,394 --> 00:06:38,249
they even woke up a pair of thrusters

171
00:06:38,269 --> 00:06:40,884
that had been considered dead for twenty-one years.

172
00:06:41,124 --> 00:06:42,197
And they fired.

173
00:06:43,277 --> 00:06:44,797
Every fix is a gamble,

174
00:06:44,817 --> 00:06:45,957
sent into the dark,

175
00:06:46,027 --> 00:06:47,997
with a two-day wait for the answer.

176
00:06:48,427 --> 00:06:50,097
As the mission manager put it,

177
00:06:50,127 --> 00:06:52,222
you have to expect the unexpected.

178
00:06:53,222 --> 00:06:54,313
So why go to all this

179
00:06:54,333 --> 00:06:55,852
trouble for an old machine?

180
00:06:56,052 --> 00:06:58,009
Because of what it has already shown us.

181
00:06:59,179 --> 00:07:00,109
Chapter six.

182
00:07:00,129 --> 00:07:01,265
Leaving the bubble.

183
00:07:01,955 --> 00:07:04,655
The Sun blows a giant bubble around itself.

184
00:07:04,985 --> 00:07:06,095
In twenty twelve,

185
00:07:06,175 --> 00:07:09,823
Voyager one became the first human-made object to leave it.

186
00:07:10,423 --> 00:07:12,493
That bubble is made of the solar wind,

187
00:07:12,613 --> 00:07:15,133
a stream of particles pouring off the Sun.

188
00:07:15,613 --> 00:07:18,133
On August the twenty-fifth, twenty twelve,

189
00:07:18,223 --> 00:07:21,103
about a hundred and twenty-two times farther out than Earth,

190
00:07:21,303 --> 00:07:24,469
Voyager crossed its edge into interstellar space.

191
00:07:25,659 --> 00:07:29,349
Now, another myth.

192
00:07:29,499 --> 00:07:29,759
Voyager one has left the solar system.

193
00:07:29,779 --> 00:07:30,369
Not quite.

194
00:07:30,639 --> 00:07:32,563
The Sun's gravity still holds a

195
00:07:32,583 --> 00:07:34,359
huge cloud of icy bodies,

196
00:07:34,379 --> 00:07:35,469
far beyond.

197
00:07:36,059 --> 00:07:38,223
Voyager needs about three hundred years to

198
00:07:38,243 --> 00:07:40,329
reach the inner edge of that cloud,

199
00:07:40,549 --> 00:07:43,351
and perhaps thirty thousand years to pass through it.

200
00:07:44,351 --> 00:07:45,641
Long before any of that,

201
00:07:45,671 --> 00:07:46,821
in nineteen ninety,

202
00:07:46,981 --> 00:07:49,693
it turned around and took one last look home.

203
00:07:50,713 --> 00:07:52,703
From six billion kilometres away,

204
00:07:52,723 --> 00:07:53,863
Earth was a speck,

205
00:07:53,963 --> 00:07:55,863
about a tenth of a single pixel.

206
00:07:56,163 --> 00:07:58,722
Carl Sagan called it the pale blue dot.

207
00:08:00,132 --> 00:08:01,838
And Voyager carries a message for

208
00:08:01,858 --> 00:08:03,402
anyone who might find it.

209
00:08:03,742 --> 00:08:04,912
A golden record,

210
00:08:04,932 --> 00:08:06,832
with a hundred and fifteen pictures,

211
00:08:07,012 --> 00:08:09,172
greetings in fifty-five languages,

212
00:08:09,222 --> 00:08:11,159
and about ninety minutes of music.

213
00:08:12,349 --> 00:08:14,149
In about forty thousand years,

214
00:08:14,239 --> 00:08:15,750
it will drift within one point

215
00:08:15,770 --> 00:08:17,509
six light-years of another star.

216
00:08:17,899 --> 00:08:18,629
By then,

217
00:08:18,709 --> 00:08:21,391
it will have been silent for a very long time.

218
00:08:22,581 --> 00:08:25,010
So, how do we still hear Voyager one?

219
00:08:25,590 --> 00:08:27,370
Because we built giant ears,

220
00:08:27,390 --> 00:08:29,920
and pointed them at exactly the right spot.

221
00:08:30,210 --> 00:08:31,940
Because we ration every watt.

222
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:34,300
And because, for forty-nine years,

223
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:37,223
people have kept fixing a machine they can never touch.

224
00:08:38,413 --> 00:08:40,103
Some time in the twenty-thirties,

225
00:08:40,123 --> 00:08:41,793
the power will run too low,

226
00:08:41,813 --> 00:08:43,243
and the whisper will stop.

227
00:08:43,463 --> 00:08:44,693
But Voyager won't.

228
00:08:44,963 --> 00:08:46,513
It will keep sailing outward,

229
00:08:46,573 --> 00:08:48,650
carrying our pictures and our music.

230
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,080
To mark the light-day,

231
00:08:51,210 --> 00:08:55,100
NASA invited people everywhere to flash V, G, R,

232
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:56,640
Voyager's call sign,

233
00:08:56,670 --> 00:08:58,626
in Morse code at the night sky.

234
00:08:59,726 --> 00:09:01,336
And here's one last number.

235
00:09:01,676 --> 00:09:03,776
In the nine minutes you've spent watching this,

236
00:09:03,876 --> 00:09:05,881
Voyager one has travelled about nine

237
00:09:05,901 --> 00:09:08,267
thousand kilometres farther from home.

238
00:09:09,447 --> 00:09:11,947
Every word it sends us today left it yesterday.

239
00:09:12,247 --> 00:09:14,177
A whisper from one light-day away,

240
00:09:14,197 --> 00:09:15,619
still saying hello.

241
00:09:17,099 --> 00:09:18,299
If you enjoyed this,

242
00:09:18,319 --> 00:09:20,659
like the video and subscribe to Whyverse.

243
00:09:20,939 --> 00:09:22,349
And for shorter stories,

244
00:09:22,459 --> 00:09:24,621
find us on Instagram and TikTok.

